THIRD-PARTY MATERIALS

The MIT license above covers the original work in this repository. Items 1
to 3 below were copied into it from elsewhere: that license does not cover
them, copyright in them stays with their respective holders, and no
additional permission to reuse them has been obtained on your behalf. Item 4
records a design influence rather than copied material.

1. Built-in agent definitions. The `description` and `tools` fields in
   agents/Explore.md and agents/Plan.md, and the `description` field in
   agents/general-purpose.md, are reproduced verbatim from Claude Code's
   built-in agents of the same name. Copyright in those fields belongs to
   Anthropic. Each of these three files overrides the built-in agent it
   shares a name with, which is why the fields are copied word for word:
   `description` is what Claude Code reads to decide when to dispatch the
   agent, and `tools` is what grants it its tool set, so editing either one
   would make these files behave differently from the built-in versions they
   replace. The body of each file is original.

2. Quoted sources in the research archives.
   skills/de-ai-writing/references/research.md and
   skills/interface-design/references/research.md quote short passages from
   third-party works, among them Nielsen Norman Group articles, Edward
   Tufte, George Orwell, Ted Chiang, Paul Graham, 余光中, 汪曾祺 and Verlyn
   Klinkenborg. Those are the most substantial sources, not the full set:
   every quotation is attributed where it appears, and the complete source
   list sits at the end of each file. Copyright in the quoted passages stays
   with their authors, and no reprint permission has been obtained on your
   behalf — quoting any of them again in your own work rests on the same
   fair-quotation footing these archives rely on, which is yours to judge.
   Which passages were selected, how they were translated, and the analysis
   written around them are original and fall under the MIT license above.

3. Color values. statusline-command.sh reproduces eight color values from
   the Gruvbox palette by Pavel Pertsev, published under the MIT license.
   The eight values are listed in the comments at the top of that file.

4. Design influence. The review sequence in skills/de-ai-writing/ was
   informed by the six-step flow of the shuorenhua skill
   (github.com/MrGeDiao/shuorenhua). No text was copied and the sequence
   here was written independently; the citation sits in the source list of
   skills/de-ai-writing/references/research.md.
